            "result_text": "Perfect! The regex has been successfully created and tested. Here's a summary of what the regex does:\n\n## Regex Features\n\nThe regex in `/app/regex.txt` matches dates in YYYY-MM-DD format from log lines that contain an IPv4 address, selecting only the **last date** if multiple dates appear on the same line.\n\n**Key Components:**\n\n1. **IPv4 Validation**: Matches 0-255 in each octet without leading zeros\n2. **Date Validation** (with month-specific day ranges):\n   - 31-day months (Jan, Mar, May, Jul, Aug, Oct, Dec): 01-31\n   - 30-day months (Apr, Jun, Sep, Nov): 01-30\n   - February: 01-29 (allowing Feb 29 without leap-year validation)\n3. **Boundary Protection**: Uses negative lookbehind/lookahead to prevent false matches with adjacent alphanumeric characters\n4. **Last Date Selection**: Negative lookahead ensures no other valid date follows the matched date\n5. **Full Line Matching**: Uses `^` and `$` anchors to match complete lines with MULTILINE flag\n\n**Test Results** verify it correctly:\n- ✓ Matches dates on lines with IPv4\n- ✓ Selects only the last date when multiple dates present\n- ✓ Rejects invalid dates (Feb 30, Apr 31, etc.)\n- ✓ Avoids false matches (e.g., \"1134-12-1234\" is not treated as a date)\n- ✓ Handles edge cases (min/max IPs, dates at line start)\n\nThe regex is ready to use with Python's `re.findall(pattern, log_text, re.MULTILINE)`."
